#!/sbin/openrc-run
# infra-os:   alpine
# infra-init: openrc
# infra-path: /etc/init.d
# infra-name: cloudflared
# infra-mode: 0755
#
# HAND-WRITTEN, AND IT HAS TO BE. Alpine packages no init script for cloudflared —
# docs/host-setup.md installs the binary straight from GitHub — so this was assembled
# from the Alpine package source and other references. Whatever preceded it DID NOT
# START ON REBOOT.
#
# That is the warning worth carrying, and it is not "the generated service errors".
# `cloudflared service install` produces something that looks correct, starts when you
# start it, and then does not come back after a reboot — on a remote box, discovered
# whenever the box next restarts. Do not replace this file with it.
#
# LOGGING, FIXED 2026-08-23. This previously declared stdout_log and stderr_log, which
# supervise-daemon does not act on. /var/log/cloudflared.err was last written on
# 2026-01-04 and cloudflared.log had been 0 bytes since 2025-12-24, through a service
# that restarts regularly. On `zero` six consecutive crashes produced no output at all
# and had to be diagnosed by reproducing them in the foreground.
#
# The names below are copied from /etc/init.d/docker ON THIS HOST: a stock Alpine
# script, same supervisor, in the default runlevel, that demonstrably writes its log.
# `output_logger` takes a COMMAND rather than a path, which is why the old declaration
# was silently inert rather than wrong-looking.

# AUTOUPDATE IS ON, AND STAGGERED. `--autoupdate-freq` is how often this host CHECKS;
# it always takes the newest, so a host that checks rarely is simply behind. The fleet
# is one -> two -> zero:
#
#     one    6h      the non-critical box, sees a release within hours
#     two   72h      the lifeboat, ~1.5 days behind
#     zero 240h      Immich and everyone's way in, ~5 days behind
#
# This host: 6h0m0s (hours). Change it in step with the other two or the order is gone.
#
# WHAT THIS BUYS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It buys time: a release that breaks `one` has
# days to be noticed before it reaches `zero`. It does NOT guarantee order — zero's check
# can land just after a release, a few percent per release at these numbers — and there is
# no health gate: a bad version reaches all three eventually. A gated rollout exists in
# ansible/playbooks/cloudflared-update.yml and is not used, decided 2026-08-23: it needs
# a human free to run it, and the reason updates were not happening was exactly that
# nobody was. Revisit once uptime monitoring exists and can be the gate.
#
# Autoupdate was briefly turned off the same day, on the grounds that it replaces a
# boot-path binary with unverified bytes. True, and accepted: HTTPS to the vendor's
# release URL is what the updater does, hash-pinning defends against a compromised
# release, and the threat actually in play is an unreachable fleet because nobody had
# time to ssh in. cloudflared's own updater is also vendor code that already runs,
# which CLAUDE.md prefers over new unattended code whose failure is the tunnel not
# coming back.

cfgfile="/etc/$RC_SVCNAME/$RC_SVCNAME.conf"
supervisor=supervise-daemon
command="/usr/bin/cloudflared"
command_args="--autoupdate-freq 6h0m0s --config /etc/cloudflared/config.yml tunnel run"
command_user="root"
pidfile="/var/run/$RC_SVCNAME.pid"
command_background="yes"

# NO --token. The secret is /etc/cloudflared/one.json at 0600, referenced by config.yml,
# so it is no longer in argv and no longer reaches syslog on every start. Changed
# 2026-08-23, after the same change on zero.
#
# `cfgfile` and `command_background` both appear vestigial — nothing here references
# either, and docker's script sets neither. Left alone rather than removed in the same
# change as the logging fix; one thing at a time on a boot-path file.

# ONE file, not two. The old split produced a 397 KB .err and a .log that had been
# empty for eight months, because cloudflared writes essentially everything to stderr.
# `-m` merges the streams, which is what docker does when both point at one place.
CF_LOGFILE="${CF_LOGFILE:-/var/log/${RC_SVCNAME}.log}"
LOGPROXY_OPTS="$LOGPROXY_OPTS -m"

# 0640 rather than docker's 0644. A deliberate tightening, not a copying error: this
# service has now twice left a world-readable file containing its own credential on
# this fleet, so its log does not get world-read by default either. It should contain
# no secret — cloudflared never prints its token — but that was also true of the init
# script, right up until it wasn't.
export \
	LOGPROXY_CHMOD="${LOGPROXY_CHMOD:-0640}" \
	LOGPROXY_LOG_DIRECTORY="${LOGPROXY_LOG_DIRECTORY:-/var/log}" \
	LOGPROXY_ROTATION_SIZE="${LOGPROXY_ROTATION_SIZE:-104857600}" \
	LOGPROXY_ROTATION_TIME="${LOGPROXY_ROTATION_TIME:-86400}" \
	LOGPROXY_ROTATION_SUFFIX="${LOGPROXY_ROTATION_SUFFIX:-.%Y%m%d%H%M%S}" \
	LOGPROXY_ROTATED_FILES="${LOGPROXY_ROTATED_FILES:-5}"

output_logger="log_proxy $LOGPROXY_OPTS $CF_LOGFILE"
error_logger="log_proxy $LOGPROXY_OPTS $CF_LOGFILE"

depend() {
	need net
}
